.TH SAFEHALT 8 "August 2026" "SafeHalt 0.3.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
safehalt \- emergency isolation and reversible file quarantine for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo safehalt
{setup|test|status|doctor|trigger|paths|recovery|network-recover}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SafeHalt
uses two independent emergency passwords. The lockdown password isolates
networking, locks local sessions and requests a clean power-off through
capability-detected backends.
The quarantine password first moves explicitly allowlisted paths into a
root-only same-filesystem quarantine and then performs the lockdown sequence.
SafeHalt does not erase files, disks, partitions or encryption keys.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup [--force]
Configure or replace both emergency passwords.
.TP
.B test
Identify a password's mode without changing the system.
.TP
.B status
Audit dependencies, configured paths, manifests and LUKS detection.
.TP
.B doctor [--json]
Report distribution metadata, init system, selected backends and whether the
complete lockdown sequence is supported. Distribution metadata is not used to
authorize system actions.
.TP
.B paths add PATH
Add an existing, non-root-owned subdirectory below a user's home.
.TP
.B paths remove PATH
Remove an exact target from the allowlist.
.TP
.B paths list
List configured targets.
.TP
.B trigger
Prompt once; the supplied emergency password selects lockdown or quarantine.
.TP
.B recovery list
List recorded quarantine activations.
.TP
.B recovery run ACTIVATION_ID
Restore one activation after verifying the quarantine password.
.TP
.B network-recover
Remove SafeHalt network isolation after verifying the quarantine password.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/safehalt/credentials.json
scrypt-derived password records, root-owned with mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/safehalt/paths.json
Explicit quarantine allowlist.
.TP
.I /var/lib/safehalt/manifests
Root-only recovery manifests.
.TP
.I HOME_PARENT/.safehalt-quarantine
Root-only reversible quarantine data, created separately for each account home
root represented by the configured paths.
.SH SECURITY
Complete user homes, mount points, symbolic links, overlapping targets and
paths outside declared non-root account homes are rejected. Quarantine requires
an atomic rename on the same filesystem. An attacker with root can bypass SafeHalt. Confidentiality
after shutdown additionally depends on full-disk encryption.
